messages:
sam-sales: Heads-up — FinCorp POC reports truncated streaming responses from our TS SDK when routed through their corporate proxy. Client shows partial chunks then an abrupt `ECONNRESET` and application logs a 504 after ~30s. They say same client works with other vendors. Any quick triage steps?

elaine-se: ouch. first things: which SDK & version? hosted or privatelink? also ask if they have an outbound HTTP(S) proxy doing TLS interception (MITM) or content-scanning. corporate appliances often buffer chunked responses and close idle sockets.

sam-sales: they're on node sdk 1.2.0, hosted (eu-central). proxy = SecureMesh v3. client-side team can reproduce consistently.

dev-hendricks: common checklist: enable keepAlive in your http agent, lower socket idle thresholds, and add a short per-chunk timeout with automatic resume (idempotent). i'll paste a minimal TS snippet to try.

```js
// minimal: force keepAlive + shorter socket timeout
const http = require('http')
const keepAliveAgent = new http.Agent({ keepAlive: true, keepAliveMsecs: 20000 })
const client = new Redwood.Client({ apiKey: process.env.RW_KEY, httpAgent: keepAliveAgent, requestTimeoutMs: 45000, maxRetries: 3 })

// streaming usage
const stream = await client.stream({ model: 'rwd-stream-1', prompt: '...' })
for await (const chunk of stream) {
  handle(chunk)
}
```

dev-hendricks: Python alt if they have a python worker: set `socket_options` to enable TCP keepalive and shorter probe intervals. Also set `stream_chunk_timeout_ms=15000`.

elaine-se: ask them to run with DEBUG logs and paste the snippet showing the socket reset. look specifically for `upstream reset by peer` vs `502 from proxy` — difference matters. if proxy emits 502/504 it's the middlebox, if we see no backend error it's likely the proxy.

sam-sales: they sent logs: proxy returns 502 after 28s; our backend traces show no error and steady token emission. customer asks whether Dedicated/Privatelink avoids this. procurement also wants an estimate.

dev-hendricks: yes, a privatelink or dedicated setup that bypasses public egress usually avoids corporate middleware entirely. for a small POC (single reserved instance) typical ballpark is ~$6.5k/mo + onboarding — sales should confirm exact SKU.

elaine-se: short-term options: 1) have them whitelist our host and disable proxy inspection for the POC, 2) use the keepAlive + chunk timeout patch and add 2x client-side retries to re-open stream on recoverable errors, 3) if they can't change proxy, recommend privatelink for the POC.

infra-bot: streaming runtime eu-central rolling restart completed at 2812345900 :white_check_mark:

sam-sales: Action items: (A) I'll send the TS snippet and ask them to run with DEBUG=redwood:* and return the 10s before failure. (B) I'll loop in procurement on the privatelink estimate. (C) Elaine, can you draft a 1-paragraph sec note on why privatelink mitigates MITM risks for their security team?

elaine-se: sure — i'll draft the note and attach our sec questionnaire. also include instructions for short-term whitelist for the POC.

dev-hendricks: if their logs still show 502/504 and our traces show tokens emitted, we'll mark this as middlebox and recommend privatelink ASAP. if we see backend throttles/429 we'll look at request patterns and advise batching/limit changes.

sam-sales: thanks all 44d i'll follow up once they run the snippet and share logs.
